* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - reads a version 2 SCRAM record, and compares in
*           constant time.  docs/SCRAM_AUTH.md
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - the credential store is $cred on disk.  5.12 (a).
* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - written for the account credential model
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !CRED_VERIFY(account, password, ok)
*
*    Returns ok = @true only if the account has a credential recorded and the
*    password derives to the stored StoredKey.
*
* THIS SURVIVES SCRAM, WHICH THE DESIGN NOTE FIRST SAID IT WOULD NOT.  SCRAM
* itself has no "verify this password" step - the client proves knowledge of
* the password without sending it, so the server is never handed one to check.
* But MODIFY.PASSWORD is: changing your own password requires the current one,
* and there the password IS in hand, locally, in an elevated session.  So the
* routine keeps its signature and its callers, and only what it reads changes.
*
* It leaves the API login path rather than disappearing.  APISRVR calls it
* until the SCRAM exchange replaces that path; after then this exists for
* MODIFY.PASSWORD alone.
*
* FAIL CLOSED, AND THAT INCLUDES OLD RECORDS.  A version 1 record - Argon2
* verifier, no StoredKey - cannot be checked here and cannot be converted
* without the plaintext, which was never stored.  It is refused rather than
* accommodated, and the account has to run MODIFY.PASSWORD again.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine CRED_VERIFY(account, password, ok)
$catalog !CRED_VERIFY

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

   ok = @false
   set.status 0

   account = upcase(trim(account))
   if account = '' or password = '' then return

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end

   read cred.rec from cred.f, account else
      close cred.f
      return                     ;* no credential - fail closed
   end

   close cred.f

   if cred.rec<CRED$VERSION> # SCRAM$CRED.VERSION then return

   salt = cred.rec<CRED$SALT>
   iter = cred.rec<CRED$ITER>
   stored.key = cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY>
   if salt = '' or iter = '' or stored.key = '' then return

   salted = SDEXT(password : @FM : salt : @FM : iter : @FM : SCRAM$KEY.LEN, @TRUE, SD_PBKDF2)
   if salted = '' then return

   client.key = SDEXT(salted : @FM : 'Client Key', @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)
   derived = SDEXT(client.key, @TRUE, SD_SHA256)

   salted = ''
   client.key = ''

   if derived = '' then return

*  Constant time, where version 1 used BASIC's "=".  That comparison returned
*  as soon as two bytes differed, which leaks how much of a guess was right.
*  Faint over a network, but it costs nothing to remove and the primitive was
*  written for exactly this call.
   if SDEXT(derived : @FM : stored.key, @TRUE, SD_CTEQUAL) = '1' then ok = @true

   return
end

* END-CODE
